WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.373 --> 00:00:03.660
- I descend from the sky and
land on things really hard

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.519
and I go at everything
with that amount of fury.

00:00:06.519 --> 00:00:09.284
Every damn person who said
I wouldn't be anything,

00:00:09.284 --> 00:00:11.403
I'm crushing them every day.

00:00:11.403 --> 00:00:12.244
What are you doing tomorrow?

00:00:12.244 --> 00:00:13.438
Eight o'clock on stage.

00:00:13.438 --> 00:00:14.271
What are you doing the night after?

00:00:14.271 --> 00:00:15.229
I'll be on stage.

00:00:15.229 --> 00:00:16.104
What about two months from now?

00:00:16.104 --> 00:00:18.160
I'll be still on that same tour.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.680
- He's an American musician,
actor, writer, and comedian.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.214
He fronted the hardcore
California punk band Black Flag.

00:00:25.214 --> 00:00:26.561
He hosts a weekly radio show

00:00:26.561 --> 00:00:28.838
and is a regular columnist for LA Weekly.

00:00:28.838 --> 00:00:30.833
He's Henry Rollins and here's my take

00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:32.571
on his top 10 rules of success.

00:00:32.571 --> 00:00:34.374
Rule number six is my personal favorite,

00:00:34.374 --> 00:00:36.750
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:39.236
and as always guys, as
you're watching the videos,

00:00:39.236 --> 00:00:41.015
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:41.015 --> 00:00:42.871
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:42.871 --> 00:00:44.001
and put quotes around it so other

00:00:44.001 --> 00:00:45.959
people can be inspired as well.

00:00:45.959 --> 00:00:48.131
Also as you are writing something down,

00:00:48.131 --> 00:00:51.078
it's much more likely to
stick in your head too.

00:00:51.078 --> 00:00:51.911
Enjoy.

00:00:54.439 --> 00:00:57.189
(dramatic music)

00:01:05.037 --> 00:01:06.687
- You once said in an
interview that your life

00:01:06.687 --> 00:01:09.927
is paranoia and fear and you're
just trying to not get hit.

00:01:09.927 --> 00:01:11.070
- Yeah.
- Is that still true?

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:11.955
- Yeah.

00:01:11.955 --> 00:01:15.673
Anger and fear, fear of failure drives me.

00:01:15.673 --> 00:01:19.585
Paranoia of things going
wrong around me drives me.

00:01:19.585 --> 00:01:24.038
I am driven by a lot of like
really kind of ugly anger,

00:01:24.038 --> 00:01:25.868
and so I have a lot of that in me,

00:01:25.868 --> 00:01:28.280
and it comes from a
father who was terrifying

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.163
and teachers who yelled at me,
told me I'd never be anything

00:01:31.163 --> 00:01:35.714
and so I descend from the sky
and land on things really hard

00:01:35.714 --> 00:01:38.720
and I go at everything
with that amount of fury,

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:40.411
and I'm not saying I'm
going to hit anybody,

00:01:40.411 --> 00:01:41.283
and I'm not mad at you.

00:01:41.283 --> 00:01:43.104
I'm quite happy to be here with you

00:01:43.104 --> 00:01:45.252
and answer any question
to the best of my ability,

00:01:45.252 --> 00:01:47.777
but I go at everything
like you think I can't.

00:01:47.777 --> 00:01:48.807
Hey, you want to try acting?

00:01:48.807 --> 00:01:50.170
Yeah, I'll try acting.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:51.124
Can you act?

00:01:51.124 --> 00:01:52.967
No, so let's go.

00:01:52.967 --> 00:01:54.482
- I've seen Sons of Anarchy, you can act.

00:01:54.482 --> 00:01:55.315
- Thank you.

00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:57.463
Fair is for losers.

00:01:57.463 --> 00:01:59.328
I'd rather win.

00:01:59.328 --> 00:02:03.276
- You tend to be incredibly
modest in terms of

00:02:03.276 --> 00:02:05.609
describing your talents, and

00:02:06.614 --> 00:02:08.394
you always seem to belittle

00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:10.187
your abilities to a certain extent

00:02:10.187 --> 00:02:13.666
when clearly there's a reason why this,

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:15.647
everything you do's been working out.

00:02:15.647 --> 00:02:18.680
I was reading about how you
said that to a certain extent

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.245
that comes from your punk rock roots.

00:02:21.245 --> 00:02:22.834
- Yeah, there was no other choice.

00:02:22.834 --> 00:02:26.043
In those days, there was no
idea of this makes money.

00:02:26.043 --> 00:02:28.126
There's no idea of like this is my future.

00:02:28.126 --> 00:02:29.353
It was like a week at a time.

00:02:29.353 --> 00:02:32.517
We have five shows, we're
going to get to Sunday. Phew.

00:02:32.517 --> 00:02:36.235
And even those days were
like fraught with turbulence.

00:02:36.235 --> 00:02:38.609
And so I never really saw a future.

00:02:38.609 --> 00:02:41.518
None of us did, and some
of us didn't make it.

00:02:41.518 --> 00:02:44.591
Some of them are dead,
and some of them are just

00:02:44.591 --> 00:02:48.895
kind of trying to recreate
their past, and so

00:02:48.895 --> 00:02:51.907
I realized that I have
to be very hard working

00:02:51.907 --> 00:02:54.801
because I don't have kind
of the ease of talent,

00:02:54.801 --> 00:02:57.648
and I come from that, and
I think in a lot of ways,

00:02:57.648 --> 00:02:59.705
it's served me very well in that I am

00:02:59.705 --> 00:03:01.901
kind of as hard working as I've ever been

00:03:01.901 --> 00:03:04.401
completely without confidence.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:06.370
I have none.

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:07.832
I don't want any.

00:03:07.832 --> 00:03:10.126
Because I think you turn
your back on the thing,

00:03:10.126 --> 00:03:11.668
and that's when you get gored,

00:03:11.668 --> 00:03:13.021
is when you think, "I got this."

00:03:13.021 --> 00:03:14.469
I never think, "I got this."

00:03:14.469 --> 00:03:17.723
I think, "Oh, man, don't screw this up."

00:03:17.723 --> 00:03:20.048
And I go at everything like including

00:03:20.048 --> 00:03:22.106
you and I sitting here right now.

00:03:22.106 --> 00:03:25.316
I'm trying to be fully engaged,

00:03:25.316 --> 00:03:28.404
and that intensity, I think has served me

00:03:28.404 --> 00:03:31.790
quite well, but it comes
from punk rock, absolutely.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:33.941
Last boss I ever had was
my boss at Haagen-Dazs

00:03:33.941 --> 00:03:36.846
at my ice cream shop, and
to this day, he still comes

00:03:36.846 --> 00:03:39.248
to my shows, and whenever I see Steve,

00:03:39.248 --> 00:03:40.975
who got me my first
apartment, who believed.

00:03:40.975 --> 00:03:42.076
I was living in my car.

00:03:42.076 --> 00:03:42.909
He believed in me.

00:03:42.909 --> 00:03:45.329
Whenever I see him, I get all weepy.

00:03:45.329 --> 00:03:47.028
I always sound like, "It's
good to see you sir."

00:03:47.028 --> 00:03:48.490
"Henry, you can call me Steve."

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:50.755
"I can't do that, sir."

00:03:50.755 --> 00:03:52.882
'Cause he trusted me with his money,

00:03:52.882 --> 00:03:54.595
and I said, "Let me run your store.

00:03:54.595 --> 00:03:56.234
"I know how to do this, I can do this."

00:03:56.234 --> 00:03:58.023
He's like, "Well, if you
screw up, you're out of here."

00:03:58.023 --> 00:04:00.120
I said, "I won't screw
up," and it ended up being,

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:02.120
I ran his store, and so,

00:04:02.963 --> 00:04:05.098
but it was anger that said,
"I'll be here all damn week.

00:04:05.098 --> 00:04:06.808
"I'll get this whole thing right."

00:04:06.808 --> 00:04:08.170
I fired a staff and rehired a bunch

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:11.206
of people, who can really work,

00:04:11.206 --> 00:04:15.132
and it was, out of anger, like
"I'll be back in four hours

00:04:15.132 --> 00:04:19.132
"to run this $3.75 an
hour job into the ground."

00:04:20.375 --> 00:04:21.990
And then I joined Black Flag, and I

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:23.680
thought I was a hard-working person.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.688
Then you meet the guys in
Black Flag, who are so driven.

00:04:26.688 --> 00:04:29.050
You see Greg Ginn work 23 hour days.

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:30.639
Like, "Greg, you're still on the phone."

00:04:30.639 --> 00:04:31.472
"I know."

00:04:31.472 --> 00:04:32.428
"Greg, when did you eat?"

00:04:32.428 --> 00:04:33.456
"I don't know."

00:04:33.456 --> 00:04:35.320
"Greg, when was the
last time you showered?"

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:37.064
"Uh, do we have a shower?"

00:04:37.064 --> 00:04:39.488
Like the guy was, we were
not going to be stopped,

00:04:39.488 --> 00:04:43.655
and I kind of go at that intensity
like kind of anything I do.

00:04:44.921 --> 00:04:46.814
Like you're a very calm,
good-looking young man.

00:04:46.814 --> 00:04:48.414
Look at me, I'm a spaz sitting next to you

00:04:48.414 --> 00:04:50.692
like, "I'm doing an interview with you."

00:04:50.692 --> 00:04:52.201
I can't help it.

00:04:52.201 --> 00:04:53.034
- Focused.

00:04:53.034 --> 00:04:54.106
- I'm sweating, man.

00:04:54.106 --> 00:04:54.939
It's so.

00:04:54.939 --> 00:04:56.074
(laughter)

00:04:56.074 --> 00:04:59.741
But I'm like this going
to the airport like.

00:05:00.882 --> 00:05:01.965
10 miles out.

00:05:03.348 --> 00:05:08.163
And so, but it's anger that
has informed kind of my life,

00:05:08.163 --> 00:05:11.069
and unflatteringly a sense of vengeance.

00:05:11.069 --> 00:05:14.004
Every damn person who said
I wouldn't be anything,

00:05:14.004 --> 00:05:16.516
I'm crushing them every day.

00:05:16.516 --> 00:05:19.679
Everybody I had to endure
in any band I was in,

00:05:19.679 --> 00:05:22.421
every day into a powder.

00:05:22.421 --> 00:05:23.310
Yeah.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:24.268
(applause)

00:05:24.268 --> 00:05:25.555
- Thank you.

00:05:25.555 --> 00:05:27.279
And I wish them no ill.

00:05:27.279 --> 00:05:29.339
I just wish to shine brighter,

00:05:29.339 --> 00:05:31.836
and if it burns my body to a crisp,

00:05:31.836 --> 00:05:33.733
I'm happy to go right now.

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:35.826
I just do my work.

00:05:35.826 --> 00:05:38.152
For me, I've always been
very utilitarian on all

00:05:38.152 --> 00:05:39.523
of this stuff, and that's what

00:05:39.523 --> 00:05:41.529
coming from punk rock gave to me.

00:05:41.529 --> 00:05:43.456
Well I don't feel like I'm anybody,

00:05:43.456 --> 00:05:45.821
and someone wants me to sign
something, I'm like, "Sure."

00:05:45.821 --> 00:05:48.499
I don't exactly understand
why, but I'll do it for you.

00:05:48.499 --> 00:05:49.732
I don't think I'm anything.

00:05:49.732 --> 00:05:51.129
- Maybe it's also, not just because

00:05:51.129 --> 00:05:52.512
of the punk rock mentality.

00:05:52.512 --> 00:05:53.808
It's also because you were scooping

00:05:53.808 --> 00:05:55.137
ice cream not so long ago,

00:05:55.137 --> 00:05:57.375
and maybe there's an element
of insecurity in you that

00:05:57.375 --> 00:06:00.829
unless you keep working and keep striving,

00:06:00.829 --> 00:06:02.734
you could end up back there.

00:06:02.734 --> 00:06:04.994
- Oh I kind of figure that's eventual.

00:06:04.994 --> 00:06:08.346
I keep moving forward boldly
because I have nothing to lose.

00:06:08.346 --> 00:06:09.656
I'm nobody from nowhere.

00:06:09.656 --> 00:06:10.807
You should really understand that.

00:06:10.807 --> 00:06:12.577
I am from the minimum wage working world

00:06:12.577 --> 00:06:16.488
a hundred years ago, but I
don't ever have it in my head

00:06:16.488 --> 00:06:19.569
that that's any less, any
more than one tour away

00:06:19.569 --> 00:06:23.402
of coming back, and so
I just, I like to work.

00:06:24.241 --> 00:06:25.731
It's not a money or fame or anything.

00:06:25.731 --> 00:06:27.975
It's about activity and challenge.

00:06:27.975 --> 00:06:28.975
I was a very

00:06:30.634 --> 00:06:33.432
headstrong young person, and by

00:06:33.432 --> 00:06:34.432
1983 or four

00:06:36.605 --> 00:06:39.129
had started my own
publishing company to publish

00:06:39.129 --> 00:06:42.789
my own books, realizing that
no one else'll publish me,

00:06:42.789 --> 00:06:44.860
and I was right about that,

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:48.061
and so my first book,
I saved my food money

00:06:48.061 --> 00:06:50.357
from Black Flag, a little
here, little there,

00:06:50.357 --> 00:06:54.029
and I saved enough to make
a fold and staple book.

00:06:54.029 --> 00:06:56.772
I couldn't type, so I
handwrote out the things,

00:06:56.772 --> 00:06:58.724
and I made a dummy that I could

00:06:58.724 --> 00:07:01.307
offset, print, fold and staple.

00:07:02.204 --> 00:07:05.833
And I sold them for $2,
mainly gave them away,

00:07:05.833 --> 00:07:07.915
but I sold enough, and they blew out,

00:07:07.915 --> 00:07:10.736
500 just went, and then I took that money

00:07:10.736 --> 00:07:13.215
to make another print run of that,

00:07:13.215 --> 00:07:16.477
and from that money, I
made my first paperback,

00:07:16.477 --> 00:07:18.060
and 33 years later,

00:07:21.037 --> 00:07:23.109
that's a fully staffed company

00:07:23.109 --> 00:07:26.678
with books in translation
and hardcover editions

00:07:26.678 --> 00:07:29.181
and the whole, you know
the whole nine yards,

00:07:29.181 --> 00:07:31.830
but it started with, I'm
going to do my thing.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:35.846
I'll be City Lights
Junior without the talent,

00:07:35.846 --> 00:07:36.679
and that's

00:07:38.067 --> 00:07:40.866
I did that at the same
time I was doing my first

00:07:40.866 --> 00:07:44.272
spoken word gigs, and why
did I have the gumption

00:07:44.272 --> 00:07:47.128
and the audacity to start
my own book company?

00:07:47.128 --> 00:07:49.225
Because I come from punk rock.

00:07:49.225 --> 00:07:50.763
I come from watching Ian MacKaye

00:07:50.763 --> 00:07:55.052
invent Dischord Records on
his mom's kitchen table.

00:07:55.052 --> 00:07:59.219
I come from folding and gluing
picture sleeves together.

00:08:00.465 --> 00:08:01.873
Black Flag had their own label.

00:08:01.873 --> 00:08:03.589
Of course you do it yourself.

00:08:03.589 --> 00:08:06.103
You want to make a book,
sit down and write it,

00:08:06.103 --> 00:08:07.398
then go to the printing place.

00:08:07.398 --> 00:08:08.231
Where's a printer?

00:08:08.231 --> 00:08:09.898
Get in the phone book and find a printer.

00:08:09.898 --> 00:08:11.346
We make records, make some books.

00:08:11.346 --> 00:08:13.875
It was, you just do it.

00:08:13.875 --> 00:08:14.763
It's like you don't even think about it.

00:08:14.763 --> 00:08:16.171
You just do it, and I'm sure that

00:08:16.171 --> 00:08:19.171
writing is just awful, but I did it,

00:08:20.293 --> 00:08:22.964
and without even hesitation
I'm making my own book company.

00:08:22.964 --> 00:08:24.631
Stop me. I dare you.

00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:28.425
I never thought about it twice.

00:08:28.425 --> 00:08:32.362
Just because DIY man, like
let your voice be heard.

00:08:32.362 --> 00:08:35.295
My last straight job I had was 1981.

00:08:35.295 --> 00:08:37.696
Head manager of a
Haagen-Dazs in Georgetown,

00:08:37.696 --> 00:08:40.109
in Washington, D.C. on Wisconsin
Avenue at the intersection

00:08:40.109 --> 00:08:44.564
of Wisconsin and O Street,
making $3.75 an hour, $4 an hour,

00:08:44.564 --> 00:08:47.941
something like that, working
40 to 60 hours a week.

00:08:47.941 --> 00:08:49.210
I had a small apartment, which I

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:51.411
shared with an old pal of mine.

00:08:51.411 --> 00:08:53.882
I had a small record collection,

00:08:53.882 --> 00:08:55.882
an ailing VW automobile,

00:08:58.496 --> 00:09:01.598
and a little tiny life
with my minimum wage job,

00:09:01.598 --> 00:09:05.494
which I liked, and I looked
at my life and realized,

00:09:05.494 --> 00:09:08.489
this is probably about as good
as it's going to get for me.

00:09:08.489 --> 00:09:10.342
I might quit this place
and go work at my friend's

00:09:10.342 --> 00:09:12.292
record store 'cause I
liked being around records,

00:09:12.292 --> 00:09:14.573
and I could probably
run his store very well,

00:09:14.573 --> 00:09:17.431
knowing what I knew about
retail at that point,

00:09:17.431 --> 00:09:19.401
and then I got a very lucky break

00:09:19.401 --> 00:09:21.534
'cause at that same time,
I was very frustrated.

00:09:21.534 --> 00:09:24.060
I said, "Well, this is going
to be a very tough life.

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:27.309
"It's going to be a life of
a lot of standing on my feet,

00:09:27.309 --> 00:09:29.941
"a lot of work, taking
it from other people,

00:09:29.941 --> 00:09:34.893
"and having very little to show
for it by Friday, you know,

00:09:34.893 --> 00:09:38.060
"Friday evening," and then Black Flag,

00:09:39.577 --> 00:09:41.177
the famous band who was friends

00:09:41.177 --> 00:09:43.894
of mine, they played in New York.

00:09:43.894 --> 00:09:47.084
I was in Washington, I took
a ride up to go see them

00:09:47.084 --> 00:09:48.808
'cause they weren't
coming down to my town.

00:09:48.808 --> 00:09:52.327
I jumped on stage and
sang with them that night

00:09:52.327 --> 00:09:54.379
a song 'cause I had to
go back down to D.C.

00:09:54.379 --> 00:09:56.953
and go work like some awful shift.

00:09:56.953 --> 00:09:59.012
They called me a few days
later at the ice cream store

00:09:59.012 --> 00:10:01.030
and said, "You know, we're
looking for a singer,"

00:10:01.030 --> 00:10:03.166
'cause the rhythm guitar player wants to,

00:10:03.166 --> 00:10:04.653
oh I'm sorry, the vocalist wants

00:10:04.653 --> 00:10:06.460
to move down to rhythm guitar.

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:08.440
You can tell I've told this story before.

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:10.582
And we're holding auditions,
do you want a crack at this

00:10:10.582 --> 00:10:11.904
'cause we saw you onstage the other night.

00:10:11.904 --> 00:10:13.404
You're pretty wild.

00:10:13.404 --> 00:10:16.197
I looked at the ice
cream scoop in my hand,

00:10:16.197 --> 00:10:19.239
my chocolate bespattered apron,

00:10:19.239 --> 00:10:22.123
and my future in the world
of minimum wage work,

00:10:22.123 --> 00:10:24.614
or I could go up to New
York and audition for this

00:10:24.614 --> 00:10:26.232
crazy band who was my favorite.

00:10:26.232 --> 00:10:27.491
What's the worse that's
going to happen to me?

00:10:27.491 --> 00:10:30.959
I miss a day of work,
oh, there goes 21 bucks,

00:10:30.959 --> 00:10:35.126
and I get humiliated in
front of my favorite band.

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:38.884
Humiliation in young
people kind of go together.

00:10:38.884 --> 00:10:43.153
I was used to it, and so
I took a train up there,

00:10:43.153 --> 00:10:46.048
I walked into this practice
place in the east village.

00:10:46.048 --> 00:10:47.495
I'm standing there with the band

00:10:47.495 --> 00:10:48.791
with a microphone in my hand.

00:10:48.791 --> 00:10:50.543
They said, "Pick the tune,"

00:10:50.543 --> 00:10:52.372
and I sang every song they had,

00:10:52.372 --> 00:10:54.479
you know, improvising most of it.

00:10:54.479 --> 00:10:56.577
Two times through we did the set.

00:10:56.577 --> 00:10:57.770
They said, "Okay, you have a seat.

00:10:57.770 --> 00:11:01.096
"We're going to go have a band
meeting," whatever that means.

00:11:01.096 --> 00:11:01.976
And they came back like 10 minutes

00:11:01.976 --> 00:11:03.299
later and said, "Okay, you're in."

00:11:03.299 --> 00:11:04.132
I said, "What do you mean?"

00:11:04.132 --> 00:11:06.613
They said, "You're the
singer in Black Flag."

00:11:06.613 --> 00:11:07.446
I said, "So what do I do?"

00:11:07.446 --> 00:11:10.177
They go, "You quit your
job, you pack your gear,

00:11:10.177 --> 00:11:11.779
"you meet us on the road.

00:11:11.779 --> 00:11:13.420
"Here's the tour itinerary.

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:16.149
"Here's the lyrics. We'll see you soon."

00:11:16.149 --> 00:11:19.727
So fairly numb, I went
back down to Washington

00:11:19.727 --> 00:11:22.231
with this passel of lyrics in my hand,

00:11:22.231 --> 00:11:25.367
went to my boss and said,
"Uh, I'm not exactly quitting,

00:11:25.367 --> 00:11:27.462
"but here's this thing that happened."

00:11:27.462 --> 00:11:29.228
He said, "It's your shot."

00:11:29.228 --> 00:11:30.142
I said, "Yeah."

00:11:30.142 --> 00:11:31.528
He said, "Take it."

00:11:31.528 --> 00:11:35.744
I feel a genuine need to
communicate with an audience,

00:11:35.744 --> 00:11:39.807
and it might just be the
attention I didn't get as a kid.

00:11:39.807 --> 00:11:43.375
It can't come from anything good,

00:11:43.375 --> 00:11:46.639
but what I try and do is
bring the best information

00:11:46.639 --> 00:11:49.639
I can to the stage and broadcast it,

00:11:50.485 --> 00:11:54.485
so I'm, it's repoirtage
from distant points from

00:11:55.652 --> 00:11:59.196
a fairly unique standpoint,
and so basically

00:11:59.196 --> 00:12:04.046
I'm not keeping it all to
myself, but I earnestly try to,

00:12:04.046 --> 00:12:05.620
it's not about entertainment.

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:09.620
It's about communication,
warning, broadcasting,

00:12:10.537 --> 00:12:13.281
emitting, and trying to leave something

00:12:13.281 --> 00:12:17.034
of myself with these, with the audience.

00:12:17.034 --> 00:12:18.549
It's not mere entertainment.

00:12:18.549 --> 00:12:20.697
It's not just making people laugh or not.

00:12:20.697 --> 00:12:22.280
It's got to hurt me.

00:12:23.679 --> 00:12:26.284
It's got to leave lines in my face.

00:12:26.284 --> 00:12:29.114
I don't like any artistic endeavor

00:12:29.114 --> 00:12:33.571
that doesn't extol a
price on the one doing it.

00:12:33.571 --> 00:12:36.554
So I'm not looking to get
through these things okay.

00:12:36.554 --> 00:12:38.703
These things are actually very difficult

00:12:38.703 --> 00:12:42.005
and very hard on my psyche, which makes

00:12:42.005 --> 00:12:43.768
me think I'm doing the right thing.

00:12:43.768 --> 00:12:44.976
Guys like me have to do everything

00:12:44.976 --> 00:12:46.688
three times before we get it right.

00:12:46.688 --> 00:12:48.637
Thankfully I know that,
so I know that I have to

00:12:48.637 --> 00:12:51.369
get up early and try it five times because

00:12:51.369 --> 00:12:52.523
the eight year old will be able to

00:12:52.523 --> 00:12:55.469
get past it in two times, so I have to

00:12:55.469 --> 00:12:57.837
be on my feet longer,
but at least I know it.

00:12:57.837 --> 00:13:01.087
And so I just go at stuff,
and it's served me very well

00:13:01.087 --> 00:13:03.486
in that my resume's pretty varied, and

00:13:03.486 --> 00:13:05.456
I have a pretty interesting life.

00:13:05.456 --> 00:13:07.919
I get to do different stuff
and have a bit of access

00:13:07.919 --> 00:13:10.238
because I just say, "Yeah, I'll try that."

00:13:10.238 --> 00:13:13.041
I mean, why would I hold back.

00:13:13.041 --> 00:13:15.777
The repeating factors of my life have been

00:13:15.777 --> 00:13:19.133
application, discipline,
focus, repetition,

00:13:19.133 --> 00:13:20.671
you know, a gig, a gig, a gig.

00:13:20.671 --> 00:13:21.504
What are you doing tomorrow?

00:13:21.504 --> 00:13:22.637
Eight o'clock on stage.

00:13:22.637 --> 00:13:23.577
What are you doing the night after?

00:13:23.577 --> 00:13:24.410
I'll be on stage.

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:25.482
What about two months from now?

00:13:25.482 --> 00:13:29.306
I'll be still on that same
tour 'cause I manage myself.

00:13:29.306 --> 00:13:30.841
I know where I'm going.

00:13:30.841 --> 00:13:32.695
I might not finish it.

00:13:32.695 --> 00:13:34.574
I might not survive it,

00:13:34.574 --> 00:13:36.188
but I'm the one bookin' it.

00:13:36.188 --> 00:13:38.271
- Do you spend more time
thinking about the future

00:13:38.271 --> 00:13:39.104
or the past?

00:13:39.104 --> 00:13:39.937
- The future.

00:13:39.937 --> 00:13:40.770
- Why is that?

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:43.780
- Because I want it, 'cause the past

00:13:43.780 --> 00:13:46.993
holds all of your
mistakes and humiliations.

00:13:46.993 --> 00:13:49.837
The future holds whatever you can make it,

00:13:49.837 --> 00:13:51.604
and so the past is
already been determined,

00:13:51.604 --> 00:13:52.832
and it also holds all your victories

00:13:52.832 --> 00:13:54.688
and all your accomplishments, but all

00:13:54.688 --> 00:13:56.945
the bad stuff is there too, so why dwell?

00:13:56.945 --> 00:13:58.523
Learn from it and move on.

00:13:58.523 --> 00:14:00.670
Don't ignore it, and don't
say it didn't happen.

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:01.622
Don't deny it,

00:14:01.622 --> 00:14:04.455
but don't, you don't have to
dwell on it and be miserable.

00:14:04.455 --> 00:14:07.147
That happens enough to us adults already.

00:14:07.147 --> 00:14:08.442
You see someone from
your past, you're like,

00:14:08.442 --> 00:14:10.627
"Oh, it still hurts,"

00:14:10.627 --> 00:14:14.004
but the future's ready for you
to not make those mistakes,

00:14:14.004 --> 00:14:16.773
and to make it great, and
now that I have less future

00:14:16.773 --> 00:14:20.658
than I do past, I seek to
make, 'cause I'm well over

00:14:20.658 --> 00:14:22.187
the 50 yard line of mortality.

00:14:22.187 --> 00:14:23.020
(laugh)

00:14:23.020 --> 00:14:23.858
I'm 55.

00:14:23.858 --> 00:14:27.011
You know, there's no way
I'm getting 110 out of this.

00:14:27.011 --> 00:14:27.844
- I don't know.

00:14:27.844 --> 00:14:28.677
- You never know.

00:14:28.677 --> 00:14:30.011
- You seem pretty energetic, I'm saying.

00:14:30.011 --> 00:14:33.088
- Well it might just burn me out, but

00:14:33.088 --> 00:14:34.847
knowing I have less in
front of me than behind me,

00:14:34.847 --> 00:14:36.142
why not be cool with it?

00:14:36.142 --> 00:14:37.778
Don't, I'm going to die soon, like look,

00:14:37.778 --> 00:14:41.553
you got some time left, rock
out, like make it amazing,

00:14:41.553 --> 00:14:43.315
where the past has been done.

